{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"aciduric-dopey-wombat","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Discovered while resolving item 3 of story 2127-348c-c41d-472e (probe resolved-status decision).\n\nCloud's absence probe hardcodes RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES = frozenset({'Resolved','Done','Cancelled'}) in src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/probe.py. The DC side already takes its resolved-status set from config (ReconcilerConfig.resolved_statuses, src/rebar/_config_schema.py). A Cloud tenant whose workflow uses non-standard resolved-status names (e.g. 'Closed', 'Complete', 'Won''t Do') would have those issues MISCLASSIFIED as PRESENT_FILTERED instead of PRESENT_RESOLVED.\n\nDecision (recorded as a repo-artifact comment at probe.py RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES): a runtime/config hook IS warranted for Cloud↔DC parity — Cloud should read a jira.resolved_statuses config key (defaulting to the current frozenset) the same way DC reads reconciler.resolved_statuses.\n\nAcceptance: RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES is sourced from a jira.resolved_statuses config key with the current names as the default; probe classification uses the configured set; a test pins a non-standard-name tenant classifying as PRESENT_RESOLVED.","id":"e34a-1d0c-0daa-4f2c","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Cloud probe RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES should be configurable (parity with DC reconciler.resolved_statuses)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785860409841206001,"uuid":"3a62ec66-03a4-4671-9b86-6446629bb49a"}